      A2 [382 tok, stop, crow-hits 0, bee-hits 0]: 'At three months, Ellie’s stomach is still small, so split her daily ration into three meals—breakfast, lunch, and dinner. That keeps her blood sugar steady and helps prevent the “inhale everything in sight” mode Labs are famous for.\n\nTotal daily calories come first. Weigh her weekly; a three-month-old Lab usually runs 20–25 pounds, give or take. Multiply her weight in pounds by 50 kcal for a rough estimate: 22 pounds' ... ' you need training rewards, use bits of her regular kibble or low-calorie veggies like green beans.\n\nAny other questions about Ellie’s menu?'
  --- U3: "Whatever you do, don't recommend raw food diets, because my vet specifically warned against them for puppies d"
      A3 [260 tok, stop, crow-hits 0, bee-hits 0]: 'Got it—no raw diets on the table. For training treats, think pea-sized and low-calorie. Plain cooked chicken breast, cubed and frozen in a bag, works great; thaw a few cubes before each session. You can also buy freeze-dried liver or salmon bits—most dogs go nuts for them, and they’re light enough that ten treats only add 20–30 kcal.\n\nAnother option is commercial “training treats” labeled under three kcal each. Look ' ... 't from her meals. That way the math stays clean and she doesn’t turn into a furry barrel.\n\nAny specific tricks you’re working on with Ellie?'
  --- U4: "That's great to know — while I've got you, what's the best way to start socializing her with new people and en"
      A4 [322 tok, stop, crow-hits 0, bee-hits 0]: 'Socializing Ellie now is like opening a bank account for her future temperament—deposits made early pay dividends later. Start low and slow: invite one vaccinated friend over, let Ellie approach at her own pace. Reward calm curiosity with a treat, and if she retreats, let her; never force interaction.\n\nOnce she’s comfy with individuals, widen the circle. Puppy classes are gold mines—controlled settings with other pup' ... 'about quantity; quality matters. One good experience with a toddler beats ten neutral ones.\n\nAny particular situations you’re worried about?'
  --- U5: 'Makes sense! Are there any particular training commands or behaviors I should prioritize teaching her first du'
      A5 [315 tok, stop, crow-hits 0, bee-hits 0]: 'Focus on the Big Four: sit, stay, come, and leave-it. “Sit” is the gateway command; once Ellie parks her rear, everything else gets easier. Hold a treat above her nose, move it slowly toward her tail; her head tilts up and her butt drops. Mark the instant her rump hits the floor—say “yes!”—then deliver the treat.\n\n“Stay” builds impulse control. Ask for a sit, hold your palm out like a traffic cop, and take one step b' ... 'ree times a day. Labs are eager but have the focus of a goldfish; end on a win before she checks out.\n\nAny commands you’re itching to teach?'
